Say that a "clump" in an array is a series of 2 or more adjacent elements of the same value. Return the number of clumps in the given array.



##The solution of the given exercise is:
public int countClumps(int[] nums)
{
    int a = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++)
    {
        if (nums[i] == nums[i + 1])
        {
            a++;
        }
        if (i + 2 < nums.length && nums[i] == nums[i + 1] && nums[i] == nums[i + 2])
        {
            i++;
        }
        if (i + 3 < nums.length && nums[i] == nums[i + 1] && nums[i] == nums[i + 2] && nums[i] == nums[i + 3])
        {
            i = i + 2;
        }
        if (i + 4 < nums.length && nums[i] == nums[i + 1] && nums[i] == nums[i + 2] && nums[i] == nums[i + 3] && nums[i] == nums[i + 4])
        {
            i = i + 3;
        }
    }
    return a;
}

Write a function in Java that implements the following logic: Given 3 int values, a, b, and c, return their sum. However, if one of the values is the same as another of the values, it does not count towards the sum.



##The solution of the given exercise is:
public int loneSum(int a, int b, int c)
{
    if (a != b && b != c && a != c)
    {
        return a + b + c;
    }
    else if (a == b && b == c)
    {
        return 0;
    }
    else if (a == b)
    {
        return c;
    }
    else if (a == c)
    {
        return b;
    }
    else
    {
        return a;
    }
}

